[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"ACID Properties in SQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) is a set of properties that guarantee database transactions are processed reliably. These properties are crucial for maintaining data integrity and ensuring that database operations are predictable and consistent, even in the face of failures or concurrent access."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Atomicity"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Atomicity ensures that a transaction is treated as a single, indivisible unit of work. Either all changes within the transaction are applied successfully, or none are. If any part of the transaction fails, the entire transaction is rolled back, leaving the database in its original state. This 'all or nothing' principle prevents partial updates and maintains data integrity."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a bank transfer scenario. Money needs to be deducted from one account and added to another. If the deduction succeeds but the addition fails (e.g., due to insufficient funds in the target account or a system error), the deduction must be reversed to maintain atomicity."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE account_id = 123;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE account_id = 456;

-- Simulate an error (e.g., invalid account_id)
-- UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE account_id = 999; -- This would cause a rollback

COMMIT;

-- If an error occurs, you would typically use ROLLBACK instead of COMMIT
-- ROLLBACK;"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, if the second `UPDATE` statement fails (e.g., account 456 doesn't exist), the entire transaction should be rolled back, and the balance in account 123 should remain unchanged. The `START TRANSACTION`, `COMMIT`, and `ROLLBACK` statements are essential for managing atomicity."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Consistency"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consistency ensures that a transaction brings the database from one valid state to another. It maintains the integrity of the data by adhering to defined rules, constraints, and data types. If a transaction violates any of these rules, it is rolled back, preventing the database from entering an invalid state."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, if a database has a constraint that an account balance cannot be negative, a transaction that attempts to withdraw more money than available in the account should be rolled back to maintain consistency."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 500 WHERE account_id = 123;

-- Assuming account 123 has a balance of 200 initially.
-- The following will violate the constraint (balance >= 0) and cause a rollback.

COMMIT;"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this scenario, if the `accounts` table has a constraint like `CHECK (balance >= 0)`, the `UPDATE` statement will violate this constraint, and the transaction will be rolled back. The database management system (DBMS) enforces these constraints to ensure consistency."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Isolation"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Isolation controls the visibility of changes made by one transaction to other concurrent transactions. It ensures that transactions operate independently of each other, as if they were executed serially. This prevents issues like dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads, which can occur when multiple transactions access and modify the same data concurrently."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"SQL databases offer different isolation levels to balance concurrency and data integrity. Common isolation levels include Read Uncommitted, Read Committed, Repeatable Read, and Serializable. Serializable provides the highest level of isolation but can reduce concurrency."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"-- Setting the isolation level (example for PostgreSQL)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;

START TRANSACTION;

-- Perform operations within the transaction
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE account_id = 123;

-- Another transaction running concurrently will not see the changes made by this transaction
-- until it is committed.

COMMIT;"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL` statement controls the isolation level for the current transaction.  Serializable isolation ensures that the transaction operates as if it were the only transaction running on the database, preventing any interference from concurrent transactions.  Other isolation levels offer different trade-offs between isolation and concurrency."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Durability"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Durability guarantees that once a transaction is committed, the changes are permanent and will survive even system failures (e.g., power outages, crashes). The DBMS typically achieves this by writing transaction logs to persistent storage before committing the transaction. These logs can be used to recover the database to a consistent state in case of a failure."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"After a `COMMIT` statement is executed, the database system ensures that all changes are written to disk. This typically involves writing to a transaction log and then updating the data files. Redundant storage and backup mechanisms further enhance durability."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 50 WHERE account_id = 123;

COMMIT;

-- After the COMMIT, the changes are durable, even if the system crashes immediately afterwards."},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, once the `COMMIT` statement is executed, the change to the account balance is guaranteed to be persistent, even if the database server crashes immediately after. The database system uses techniques like write-ahead logging to ensure durability."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The ACID properties are fundamental to reliable database transactions. They ensure data integrity, consistency, and durability, even in the presence of failures and concurrent access. Understanding and utilizing these properties is crucial for building robust and dependable database applications."}]
